{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"tasteful-historical-slug","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe bridge has nowhere to record which tracker projects a store syncs, nor which repos each\nproject's tickets belong to. Epic `0e68-41eb-5782-4336` needs that mapping to be a property of the\n**store** rather than of any one repo, because `.bridge_state/prev_snapshot.json` is shared by every\nrepo using the store: if two repos fetched different project subsets against one shared snapshot,\neach pass would see the other's projects as vanished from the remote and diff them as deletions.\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd `.bridge_state/projects.json` beside the committed bridge state already there (`bindings.json`,\n`get_rotation.json`, `prev_snapshot.json` — all present on the `tickets` branch), owned by a new\n`projects_store.py` module rather than by extending `binding_store.py`. The two records have very\ndifferent write cadences, and keeping them separate means an operator editing the mapping never\nrewrites the large bindings record.\n\nRecord shape:\n\n```json\n{\"version\": 1, \"legacy_default\": \"REB\", \"projects\": {\"REB\": {\"repos\": [\"rebar\"]}}}\n```\n\nThe `projects` key set **is** the store's sync list, so there is no second list to keep in agreement.\n`legacy_default` is the project that tickets predating this epic resolve to.\n\nThis choice sits well with ADR 0060 (\"Keep bridge state committed, but make its formats cheap\"): that\nADR's constraint is per-pass churn on the tickets branch — it measured 12.79 KB per changed\n`bindings.json` version and ~4.5 MB/day for `prev_snapshot.json`. The mapping changes only on an\noperator edit, so it adds no per-pass growth at all.\n\nSurface it as `bridge projects` under the verb group established by story `4752-3afb-e8bd-448a`\n(`src/rebar/_cli/_bridge_commands.py`), with the library and MCP parity epic `2f79-2b32-28c5-4eb3`\nrequires of every bridge verb — the bridge library surface is `src/rebar/_lib_ops.py` (`bridge_fsck`\nat `:75`) and the bridge MCP surface is `src/rebar/_mcp_reads.py`. Sub-verbs: `list`,\n`set <KEY> --repos a,b`, `remove <KEY>`.\n\nThis story also owns `resolve_project(ticket)` — the single helper implementing the tri-state rule\n(absent → `legacy_default`; explicit empty → not synced; explicit key → that key) — so the inbound\nand outbound stories consume one implementation instead of each re-deriving it.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/projects_store.py` (new)\n- `src/rebar/_cli/_bridge_commands.py`\n- `src/rebar/_lib_ops.py`\n- `src/rebar/_mcp_reads.py`\n- tests under `tests/`\n\n## Testing\n\nTier per the shared standard's CLI row — a real CLI subprocess with a persisted-state assertion —\nbecause the mechanism spans argument parsing, the on-disk JSON record, and exit codes; an in-process\nunit test would not cross the parsing or exit-code components.\n\n- Happy path: `bridge projects set REB --repos rebar` in a fresh store, then `bridge projects list`\n  returns the entry and `.bridge_state/projects.json` on disk contains it.\n- Cross-surface contract test: an entry written through the library surface is read back byte-equal\n  through the CLI and through MCP — proving one record, not three parallel stores.\n- Failure path: `bridge projects remove NOPE` on a store without that key exits non-zero, names the\n  key, and leaves the record byte-unchanged.\n- Edge: `set REB --repos` with an empty list is accepted and stores `[]` — a project with no default\n  repo is legal, not an error.\n- Collateral invariant: after any mapping write, `bindings.json` and `prev_snapshot.json` hash\n  identically to before.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `bridge projects set|list|remove` exist under the bridge verb group and operate on\n      `.bridge_state/projects.json`; checked by a CLI subprocess test asserting the on-disk record\n      after each verb.\n- [ ] The library and MCP surfaces read and write the same record as the CLI; checked by a test that\n      writes through one surface and reads the identical entry through the other two.\n- [ ] `resolve_project` returns the legacy default for an absent field, a not-synced result for an\n      explicit empty value, and the key itself for a set value; checked by a parameterized unit test\n      covering all three states.\n- [ ] A mapping write leaves `bindings.json` and `prev_snapshot.json` byte-identical; checked by\n      hashing both before and after in the CLI test.\n- [ ] `remove` of an unknown key exits non-zero and mutates nothing; checked in the same test.","id":"c927-0a68-4465-42a2","parent_id":"0e68-41eb-5782-4336","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"bridge projects: store-level project-to-repos mapping and its CLI, library and MCP surface"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786160042328371001,"uuid":"14daaaff-d4fa-4109-8817-1df3f6d6b2d1"}